export const F1={title:'The event, first aid foundations and emergency moves',hook:'About 350,000 cardiac arrests happen outside hospitals in the United States each year, and most occur at home. Survival depends on bystanders who recognize the emergency, call for help, start CPR and use an AED. CPR/First Aid is a two-person team event that prepares you to be that bystander—and to handle bleeding, burns, choking, allergic reactions and more.',objectives:['Describe the CPR/First Aid event, its test plan and its skills.','Explain the chain of survival and the legal principles of first aid.','Check a scene, use PPE and call EMS with the right information.','Choose and perform emergency moves when a patient must be moved.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'CPR/First Aid is a **team event for two competitors**. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes** (team average): **Basic Life Support 40%** (general concepts, CPR for adults, children and infants, AED use, CPR with an advanced airway, rescue breathing and choking relief) and **First Aid 60%** (basic, medical, injury and environmental emergencies). References are the **American Red Cross** BLS and First Aid/CPR/AED manuals, the **American Heart Association** BLS and Heartsaver First Aid materials and *DHO: Health Science*. Skills follow the **AHA guidelines released February 2026** and **ARC guidelines released December 2025**; teams state which organization they follow.',
'**Round Two** is a **12-minute scenario** in which **Rescuer 1** begins a BLS skill—**I** adult CPR, **II** adult CPR with AED or **III** infant CPR—while **Rescuer 2** starts a first aid skill—**IV** severe bleeding and shock, **V** choking, **VI** open fracture and splinting, **VII** severe burns, **VIII** heat-related emergency, **IX** auto-injector epinephrine or **X** naloxone nasal spray—then changes gloves and joins the BLS. Teams bring a first aid kit with **2 cravats**, bandaging materials, **mouth-to-mask and bag-mask devices (adult and infant)**, a biohazard bag, hand sanitizer, a cell phone for simulated 911 calls, bandage scissors, tape, **8 pairs of non-latex gloves**, 2 sets of goggles and a tourniquet (or use HOSA’s). Teams need **70%**; ties go to the higher average test score.',
{t:'table',caption:'Written test plan',head:['Area','Weight','Includes'],rows:[['Basic Life Support','40%','Concepts; adult, child and infant CPR; AED; advanced airway; rescue breathing; choking'],['First Aid','60%','Basic, medical, injury and environmental emergencies']]}]],
['The chain of survival',[
'The AHA’s **out-of-hospital adult chain of survival** links the actions that save lives: **(1) recognition of cardiac arrest and activation of the emergency response system**, **(2) high-quality CPR**, **(3) defibrillation**, **(4) advanced resuscitation** by EMS, **(5) post–cardiac arrest care** and **(6) recovery**. Each link depends on the one before it; a weak first link—no one calls 911 or starts CPR—breaks the chain. The in-hospital chain begins with **early recognition and prevention**.',
'Every minute without CPR and defibrillation lowers survival from sudden cardiac arrest by roughly 7–10%. Bystander CPR can **double or triple** survival. That is why the public is taught **hands-only CPR** (compressions without breaths) for adults who suddenly collapse, while healthcare-provider BLS—the level tested in this event—includes breaths, pulse checks and team roles.',
order('AHA adult out-of-hospital chain of survival',['Recognition and activation of emergency response','High-quality CPR','Defibrillation','Advanced resuscitation','Post–cardiac arrest care','Recovery'])]],
['Scene safety, consent and the law',[
'Every skill begins by **verifying scene safety**—traffic, fire, electricity, chemicals, violence or unstable structures. Put on **PPE** (gloves and eye protection) because blood and body fluids can transmit infection. Use a **mouth-to-mask** or **bag-mask** device for breaths. After care, remove gloves without touching the outside, place them in a **biohazard** bag and use hand sanitizer.',
'**Consent:** ask a conscious adult, “May I help you?” **Implied consent** applies to unresponsive people and to children when no parent or guardian is present. A competent adult may **refuse** care. **Good Samaritan laws** protect people who give reasonable care in good faith within their training. Once you begin care, do not leave the person until someone with equal or greater training takes over (leaving is **abandonment**). Healthcare workers on duty have a **duty to act**.',
match('First aid legal terms',[['Consent','Permission from a conscious, competent person'],['Implied consent','Assumed for unresponsive people'],['Good Samaritan laws','Protect good-faith rescuers'],['Abandonment','Leaving before equal or higher help arrives'],['Duty to act','Legal obligation of on-duty responders'],['Refusal','A competent adult may decline care']])]],
['Calling EMS',[
'Call **911** (or the local emergency number) for unresponsiveness, trouble breathing, chest pain, severe bleeding, seizures, stroke signs, poisoning, severe burns, suspected spinal injury and other life-threatening problems. With two rescuers, one starts care while the other calls and gets an **AED**. Use a phone’s **speaker** so you can keep working. The rating sheets require you to tell the dispatcher: **your name, location and phone number**, and the **situation, number and condition of patients**, and to **hang up only when the dispatcher says it is OK**.',
'Dispatchers can coach CPR and send the right resources. Give clear landmarks (building, floor, room), send someone to meet EMS and unlock doors. Write down important times—when the person collapsed, when CPR started, when a tourniquet or medication was given—to report to EMS.',
order('Calling 911',['State your name','Give the exact location','Give your phone number','Describe what happened','Report the number and condition of patients','Stay on the line until told it is OK to hang up'])]],
['Emergency moves',[
'Move a patient only if the scene is **dangerous** (fire, explosion risk, traffic) or you must move them to give needed care, such as CPR on a firm surface. If a head, neck or spine injury is possible, keep the head and body in line. The **clothes drag** grasps the clothing behind the neck and pulls the person headfirst while the head rests on your forearms—useful for an unresponsive person who might have a head, neck or spinal injury, such as someone found at the **bottom of stairs**. The **blanket drag** moves a person on a blanket; the **ankle drag** pulls by the ankles over a short distance when other moves are not possible.',
'For conscious patients, the **walking assist** supports a person who can walk, the **two-person seat carry** uses rescuers’ arms as a seat for someone who cannot walk but has no spinal injury, and the **pack-strap carry** works for a conscious person who cannot walk. Use good body mechanics: lift with your legs, keep the back straight and the load close, and move smoothly.',
match('Emergency move and use',[['Clothes drag','Unresponsive person with possible head, neck or spine injury'],['Blanket drag','Moving a person on a blanket in an emergency'],['Ankle drag','Short distance when other moves are not possible'],['Walking assist','Person who can walk with support'],['Two-person seat carry','Conscious person who cannot walk, no spinal injury'],['Pack-strap carry','Conscious person carried on one rescuer’s back']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Stairway fall',{a:{text:'You find an unresponsive older adult at the bottom of a staircase in a building where smoke is starting to spread. (Scenario invented for practice.) Which move is best?',choices:[['Clothes drag, keeping the head supported','b','Correct. It protects a possible spinal injury.',true],['Two-person seat carry','b','That move does not support the head and spine.',false]]},b:{text:'Outside, your partner calls 911. What must the caller tell the dispatcher?',choices:[['Name, location, phone number and the situation, number and condition of patients','c','Correct. Stay on the line until told to hang up.',true],['Only “Come quickly”','c','Dispatchers need specific information.',false]]},c:{text:'You check breathing and a pulse while your partner gets an AED.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Team of two; test: BLS 40% and First Aid 60%; Round Two is a 12-minute scenario where Rescuer 1 starts BLS and Rescuer 2 starts a first aid skill, then joins the BLS; state AHA or ARC.','Chain of survival (AHA adult out-of-hospital): recognition and activation, high-quality CPR, defibrillation, advanced resuscitation, post–cardiac arrest care, recovery.','Scene safety, PPE, consent (implied if unresponsive), Good Samaritan laws, no abandonment; 911 call: name, location, phone, situation and patients; clothes drag for possible spinal injury.'],terms:[['Chain of survival','The linked actions that improve survival from cardiac arrest.'],['Implied consent','Assumed permission to help an unresponsive person.'],['Clothes drag','An emergency move that pulls a person by the clothing while supporting the head.']],mcqs:[q('What is the second link in the AHA BLS out-of-hospital adult chain of survival?','High-quality CPR','Advanced resuscitation','Post-cardiac arrest care','Defibrillation','HOSA sample question.'),q('Pat finds an unresponsive elderly person at the bottom of a staircase. Which emergency move would be used to move the patient to assess them further?','Clothes drag','Blanket drag','Ankle drag','Two person seat carry','HOSA sample question.'),q('What percentage of the test is First Aid?','60%','40%','10%','90%','BLS is 40%.'),q('When does implied consent apply?','When the person is unresponsive','When the person refuses','Only for adults','Never','Unresponsive people cannot answer.'),q('Leaving a patient before equal or higher help arrives is:','Abandonment','Consent','Negligence only','Duty to act','Stay until relieved.'),q('Which information must you give the 911 dispatcher?','Your name, location, phone number and the patients’ situation','Your favorite hospital','The patient’s insurance','Nothing','Then wait to hang up.'),q('When should a patient be moved before care?','When the scene is dangerous','Always','Never','Only if they ask','Otherwise treat in place.'),q('How long does Round Two allow for reading the scenario and caring for the patient?','12 minutes','5 minutes','30 minutes','60 minutes','Maximum time.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the first steps a two-rescuer team takes when they find an unresponsive person in an unsafe location, including scene safety, moving the person, consent and calling EMS.',rubric:[['Scene and PPE',['scene safety','hazard','ppe','gloves','eye protection']],['Moving the patient',['dangerous','clothes drag','head','spine','body mechanics']],['Consent and law',['implied consent','unresponsive','good samaritan','abandonment']],['Calling EMS',['911','name','location','phone','situation','number','condition','hang up','aed']]],model:'First we verify scene safety—looking for smoke, fire, traffic, electricity or unstable structures—and put on PPE, including gloves and eye protection. Because the location is unsafe, we move the person before giving care. Since they are unresponsive and could have a head, neck or spinal injury, we use the clothes drag: one rescuer grasps the clothing behind the neck and pulls the person headfirst, with the head cradled on the forearms and the body kept in line, using the legs and a straight back. Once in a safe place, we check responsiveness by tapping and shouting. Because the person is unresponsive, we have implied consent to help, and Good Samaritan laws protect us as long as we act in good faith within our training; we will not leave until EMS takes over, which would be abandonment. One rescuer immediately calls 911 on speaker, giving their name, exact location and phone number, the situation and the number and condition of patients, and stays on the line until the dispatcher says it is OK to hang up, while also sending someone for an AED. The other rescuer checks breathing and a pulse and begins CPR if needed.'},sources:[RULES,AHA_CHAIN,ARC_STEPS,AHA_CPR]};

export const F2={title:'Adult BLS: high-quality CPR with two rescuers (Skill I)',hook:'High-quality chest compressions are the single most important thing a rescuer can do for a person in cardiac arrest. Compressions that are too slow, too shallow or interrupted too often can make the difference between life and death. Skill I tests two-rescuer adult CPR when no AED is available: Rescuer 1 starts, Rescuer 2 joins with a bag-mask.',objectives:['Recognize cardiac arrest by checking responsiveness, breathing and a carotid pulse.','Perform high-quality adult chest compressions.','Give breaths with a pocket mask and a bag-mask using the E-C clamp.','Perform two-rescuer CPR with role switches following HOSA Skills I-A and I-B.'],sections:[
['Recognizing cardiac arrest: Skill I-A steps 1–5',[
'**1.** Verify **scene safety** and state which resource you follow (**ARC or AHA**). **2.** Open the first aid kit and put on PPE. **3.** Check responsiveness: **tap the shoulder and shout, “Are you OK?”** **4.** Shout to your partner to **activate the emergency response system and get an AED** (in Skill I, the judge says none is available). **5.** Check for **normal breathing and a pulse** for **at least 5 but no more than 10 seconds**.',
'Under the **AHA** approach, scan the chest for rise and fall while feeling the **carotid pulse**. Under the **ARC** approach, first open the airway to a **past-neutral** position with a **head-tilt/chin-lift** (or a **modified jaw thrust** if a head, neck or spine injury is suspected), then check breathing and pulse simultaneously. Find the carotid pulse by sliding **2–3 fingers** into the groove between the **trachea** and the neck muscles. **Gasping (agonal breathing)** is not normal breathing—treat it as no breathing.',
order('Skill I-A opening',['Verify scene safety; state ARC or AHA','Put on PPE','Tap and shout “Are you OK?”','Send partner to call and get an AED','Check breathing and carotid pulse for 5–10 seconds'])]],
['High-quality compressions: Skill I-A step 6',[
'**a.** Kneel at the patient’s **side**. **b.** Remove or move bulky clothing from the chest. **c.** Make sure the patient is **face up on a firm, flat surface**. **d.** Place the **heel of one hand** on the **center of the chest on the lower half of the breastbone**. **e.** Place the heel of the other hand on top. **f.** Keep your **arms straight** with your **shoulders directly over your hands**. **g.** Compress at **100–120 per minute**, delivering **30 compressions in 15–18 seconds**. **h.** Push at least **2 inches (5 cm)** deep. **i.** **Count aloud**. **j.** Let the chest **recoil completely** after each compression.',
'High-quality CPR means the right **rate**, **depth** and **recoil**, **minimal interruptions** (less than 10 seconds; switches in under 5 seconds on the rating sheet) and **avoiding excessive ventilation**. Leaning on the chest prevents the heart from refilling, and compressions that are too fast are often too shallow. Rescuers tire within about 2 minutes, so switch compressors every **5 cycles** (about 2 minutes).',
flip('High-quality CPR',[['Rate','100–120 compressions per minute'],['Depth (adult)','At least 2 inches (5 cm)'],['Recoil','Let the chest rise fully'],['Hand position','Lower half of the breastbone'],['Ratio','30 compressions : 2 breaths'],['Switch','Every 5 cycles, in under 5 seconds']])]],
['Breaths with a pocket mask: Skill I-A steps 7–9',[
'**7.** Place the **mouth-to-mask** device on the face using the **bridge of the nose** as a guide. **8.** Seal the mask: **a.** with the hand closest to the top of the head, place your **index finger and thumb along the top edge**; **b.** place the **thumb of the other hand along the bottom edge**; **c.** place the remaining fingers along the **bony margin of the jaw** and **lift**, performing a **head-tilt/chin-lift**; **d.** press firmly around the edge to seal; **e.** give **2 breaths over 1 second each**, just enough to make the **chest rise**.',
'**9.** Continue cycles of **30 compressions and 2 breaths**, with interruptions under 5 seconds, until Rescuer 2 arrives; then tell Rescuer 2 to take over compressions. Watch for the chest to rise with each breath—if it does not, reopen the airway and try again. Too-forceful breaths can push air into the stomach, causing vomiting.',
order('Pocket-mask breaths',['Place the mask using the bridge of the nose','Index finger and thumb on the top edge','Other thumb on the bottom edge','Fingers lift the jaw; head-tilt/chin-lift','Press to seal','Give 2 breaths, 1 second each, watching chest rise'])]],
['Bag-mask ventilation and the E-C clamp: Skill I-A steps 10–14',[
'When Rescuer 2 takes over compressions, **10.** Rescuer 1 moves **directly above the patient’s head** with the **bag-mask**. **11.** Use the **E-C clamp**: **a.** perform a head tilt; **b.** place the mask using the bridge of the nose; **c.** make a **“C”** with the **thumb and index finger** on the mask, pressing the edges to the face; **d.** use the **remaining three fingers (“E”)** to lift the **angles of the jaw**, opening the airway and pressing the face into the mask. **12.** Squeeze the bag to give each breath over **1 second** after every **30 compressions**, watching for chest rise.',
'**13.** When Rescuer 2 calls for a switch after **5 cycles**, switch roles in **less than 5 seconds** and resume compressions. **14.** Continue two-person CPR at **30:2**, counting aloud, until the judge says EMS has taken over. **15–19.** Communicate, give EMS a report, remove gloves properly, place PPE in a biohazard receptacle and use hand rub.',
order('E-C clamp',['Position above the patient’s head','Head tilt','Place the mask using the bridge of the nose','Thumb and index finger form a C pressing the mask','Three fingers form an E lifting the jaw','Squeeze the bag over 1 second; watch chest rise'])]],
['Rescuer 2’s role: Skill I-B',[
'Rescuer 2 starts with a **first aid skill**, then **changes gloves**, says “**I can assist you in CPR**” and takes over **chest compressions** with the same technique: at the patient’s side, firm flat surface, heels of hands stacked on the lower half of the breastbone, arms straight, **100–120/min**, **at least 2 inches**, counting aloud and allowing full recoil.',
'Rescuer 2 **calls for the switch after 5 cycles**, then moves to the head and provides **bag-mask ventilations** with the **E-C clamp**. At the end, Rescuer 2 gives EMS information about the **first aid patient**, removes gloves properly, disposes of PPE in a biohazard receptacle and uses hand rub. Clear communication—“Switch on the next cycle,” “Breaths coming”—keeps CPR smooth.',
kase('Two-rescuer CPR',{a:{text:'Rescuer 1 has done 5 cycles of 30:2 and is getting tired. Rescuer 2 is compressing. (Scenario invented for practice.) What happens after Rescuer 2’s fifth cycle?',choices:[['Rescuer 2 calls the switch and roles change in under 5 seconds','b','Correct. Fresh compressors keep depth and rate high.',true],['Both stop for a 30-second break','b','Long pauses lower blood flow to the brain.',false]]},b:{text:'While ventilating, the chest does not rise. What should the ventilator do?',choices:[['Reposition the head and reseal the mask with the E-C clamp','c','Correct. Recheck the airway and seal.',true],['Squeeze the bag harder and faster','c','Excess force inflates the stomach.',false]]},c:{text:'The chest now rises with each breath. You continue 30:2 until EMS takes over.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Check scene safety, state ARC or AHA, PPE, tap and shout, send for 911 and AED, check breathing and carotid pulse for 5–10 seconds; gasping is not breathing.','High-quality compressions: heels of hands on the lower half of the breastbone, arms straight, 100–120/min (30 in 15–18 seconds), at least 2 inches, full recoil, count aloud, minimal interruptions.','Pocket mask then bag-mask with the E-C clamp, 2 breaths over 1 second each after every 30 compressions; switch every 5 cycles in under 5 seconds; report to EMS and remove PPE properly.'],terms:[['Agonal breathing','Gasping that is not normal breathing during cardiac arrest.'],['E-C clamp','A hand position that seals a bag-mask and lifts the jaw.'],['Chest recoil','Letting the chest return to normal position after each compression.']],mcqs:[q('What is the compression rate for adult CPR?','100–120 per minute','60–80 per minute','140–160 per minute','30 per minute','30 compressions in 15–18 seconds.'),q('How deep are adult compressions?','At least 2 inches (5 cm)','1 inch','4 inches','Half an inch','Allow full recoil.'),q('How long should you check for breathing and a pulse?','At least 5 but no more than 10 seconds','30 seconds','1 minute','2 seconds','Then act.'),q('Where is the adult pulse checked during BLS?','Carotid artery','Radial artery','Brachial artery','Femoral artery only','Groove beside the trachea.'),q('What is the adult compression-to-breath ratio?','30:2','15:2','5:1','10:1','Single or two rescuers for adults.'),q('In the E-C clamp, the C is formed by:','Thumb and index finger','Three lower fingers','The palm','The wrist','The E lifts the jaw.'),q('How often should two rescuers switch compressors?','Every 5 cycles (about 2 minutes)','Every cycle','Every 10 minutes','Never','In under 5 seconds.'),q('Each breath should be given over:','1 second','3 seconds','5 seconds','Half a second','Just enough to make the chest rise.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how two rescuers perform adult BLS/CPR without an AED following HOSA Skills I-A and I-B, from recognizing cardiac arrest to EMS taking over.',rubric:[['Recognition',['scene safety','ppe','tap','shout','activate','aed','breathing','carotid','10 seconds']],['Compressions',['firm','flat','lower half','breastbone','arms straight','100-120','2 inches','recoil','count']],['Ventilation',['pocket mask','bridge of the nose','seal','head-tilt','2 breaths','1 second','bag-mask','e-c clamp']],['Teamwork',['30:2','switch','5 cycles','5 seconds','ems','gloves','biohazard','hand rub']]],model:'Rescuer 1 verifies scene safety, states whether the team follows ARC or AHA and puts on PPE, then taps the shoulder and shouts, “Are you OK?” When there is no response, Rescuer 1 shouts for the partner to call 911 and get an AED and checks for normal breathing and a carotid pulse for 5–10 seconds, finding neither. Kneeling at the side of the patient, who is face up on a firm, flat surface, Rescuer 1 places the heel of one hand on the lower half of the breastbone with the other on top, locks the arms with the shoulders over the hands and gives 30 compressions at 100–120 per minute, at least 2 inches deep, counting aloud and letting the chest fully recoil. Rescuer 1 seals a pocket mask using the bridge of the nose as a guide, lifts the jaw with a head-tilt/chin-lift and gives 2 breaths over 1 second each, watching for chest rise, continuing 30:2 with minimal interruptions. When Rescuer 2 arrives after the first aid patient and changes gloves, they say, “I can assist you in CPR,” and take over compressions while Rescuer 1 moves to the head with a bag-mask, using the E-C clamp—thumb and index finger pressing the mask, three fingers lifting the jaw—to give 2 breaths over 1 second after every 30 compressions. Rescuer 2 calls a switch every 5 cycles, completed in under 5 seconds, and the team continues until EMS takes over, then reports to EMS, removes gloves properly, disposes of PPE in a biohazard bag and uses hand rub.'},sources:[RULES,AHA_CPR,ARC_CPR,MEDLINE_CPR]};

export const F3={title:'Adult BLS with an AED, rescue breathing and advanced airways (Skill II)',hook:'Most sudden cardiac arrests in adults begin with ventricular fibrillation—a chaotic quiver that pumps no blood. CPR keeps some blood moving, but only a shock from a defibrillator can restore a normal rhythm. Automated external defibrillators talk rescuers through every step. Skill II tests two-rescuer CPR with an AED.',objectives:['Explain how an AED works and which rhythms it treats.','Operate an AED with a partner following HOSA Skills II-A and II-B.','Handle special AED situations such as medication patches, implanted devices and water.','Provide rescue breathing and CPR with an advanced airway.'],sections:[
['How defibrillation works',[
'In **ventricular fibrillation (VF)** and **pulseless ventricular tachycardia (VT)**, the heart’s electrical activity is so disorganized that it cannot pump blood. A **defibrillator** delivers a shock that stops the chaotic activity so the heart’s natural pacemaker can restart a normal rhythm. AEDs **analyze** the rhythm and **advise a shock only for shockable rhythms**; they will not shock a normal rhythm or **asystole** (flatline).',
'AEDs are found in schools, airports, gyms and offices. Each minute without defibrillation reduces survival from VF arrest, so the AED should be used **as soon as it arrives**. Compressions continue until the AED is ready, and resume **immediately** after a shock or a “no shock advised” message—the AED will reanalyze about every 2 minutes.',
sort('Shockable or not?',['AED may advise a shock','AED will not advise a shock'],[['Ventricular fibrillation',0],['Pulseless ventricular tachycardia',0],['Asystole (flatline)',1],['Normal sinus rhythm with a pulse',1],['Chaotic quivering rhythm',0]])]],
['Using the AED: Skill II-A and II-B',[
'**Rescuer 1** begins as in Skill I: scene safety, ARC or AHA, PPE, tap and shout, send the partner for 911 and the AED, check breathing and pulse for 5–10 seconds, then begin **30:2** with high-quality compressions and pocket-mask breaths. When **Rescuer 2** arrives with the AED, Rescuer 1 **keeps compressing** while Rescuer 2 prepares it. **Rescuer 2:** **(2)** opens the case and **powers on** the AED; **(3)** applies pads to the **bare chest**—never over clothing, **medication patches** or **implanted devices**; **(4)** **clears** the patient (“Everyone clear!”) and lets the AED **analyze**; **(5)** if a shock is advised, **clears again** and **delivers the shock**.',
'**(6)** Immediately after the shock—or if no shock is advised—Rescuer 2 **begins chest compressions** while **Rescuer 1** moves to the head and gives **bag-mask breaths** with the **E-C clamp** after every 30 compressions. They **switch every 5 cycles** in under 5 seconds and continue until EMS takes over, then report, remove PPE and use hand rub. Typical adult pad placement is **upper right chest below the collarbone** and **lower left side below the armpit** (follow the pad pictures).',
order('AED sequence',['Power on the AED','Apply pads to the bare chest','Clear everyone; let the AED analyze','Clear again and shock if advised','Resume compressions immediately','Bag-mask breaths after every 30; switch every 5 cycles'])]],
['Special AED situations',[
'**Hairy chest:** press pads firmly; if the AED cannot read, quickly remove hair with a razor or a spare set of pads. **Water:** move the person out of standing water and **dry the chest** before applying pads (it is safe on wet ground or snow if the chest is dry). **Medication patches:** remove them with a gloved hand and wipe the skin. **Implanted pacemaker or defibrillator** (a hard lump under the skin, usually upper chest): place the pad at least about **1 inch away** from it. **Jewelry and piercings** near pads should be moved if possible.',
'**Children and infants:** use **pediatric pads or a pediatric setting** for children younger than about **8 years** or under **55 pounds** when available; if only adult pads are available, use them, making sure the pads do not touch—often one on the front and one on the back (**anterior–posterior**). **Never** touch the patient during analysis or shock, and never use an AED in a moving vehicle.',
match('AED situation and action',[['Standing water','Move the person and dry the chest'],['Medication patch','Remove it with gloves and wipe the skin'],['Implanted device','Place the pad about 1 inch away'],['Child under 8','Pediatric pads if available'],['Only adult pads for a child','Use them without letting them touch'],['During analysis','No one touches the patient']])]],
['Rescue breathing',[
'If a person is **not breathing normally but has a pulse**, give **rescue breaths** (respiratory arrest). For **adults**, give **1 breath every 6 seconds** (about 10 per minute). For **children and infants**, give **1 breath every 2–3 seconds** (about 20–30 per minute). Each breath lasts about **1 second** and makes the chest rise. **Recheck the pulse about every 2 minutes**; if the pulse disappears, start CPR.',
'Respiratory arrest can follow drowning, drug overdose (especially opioids), choking, asthma, electrocution or head injury. Without breathing, the heart will soon stop, so early rescue breathing can prevent cardiac arrest. For suspected opioid overdose, give **naloxone** as well.',
flip('Rescue breathing rates',[['Adult','1 breath every 6 seconds'],['Child','1 breath every 2–3 seconds'],['Infant','1 breath every 2–3 seconds'],['Breath length','About 1 second, visible chest rise'],['Pulse recheck','About every 2 minutes'],['Pulse lost','Begin CPR']])]],
['CPR with an advanced airway',[
'When trained providers place an **advanced airway**—an **endotracheal tube** or **supraglottic airway**—compressions no longer pause for breaths. Rescuers give **continuous compressions** at 100–120 per minute while delivering **1 breath every 6 seconds** (10 per minute) through the airway. This keeps blood flowing without interruptions. Excessive ventilation is harmful because it raises pressure in the chest and reduces blood return to the heart.',
'Effective teams assign clear roles—compressor, airway manager, AED operator, recorder and team leader—and use **closed-loop communication** (repeat back instructions). After a return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC), the patient needs post-arrest care in a hospital.',
kase('AED arrives',{a:{text:'Rescuer 2 arrives with an AED while Rescuer 1 is doing compressions. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should Rescuer 1 do?',choices:[['Keep compressing while Rescuer 2 turns on the AED and applies pads','b','Correct. Minimize interruptions.',true],['Stop compressions and wait for the AED','b','Stopping early lowers blood flow.',false]]},b:{text:'The AED says “Shock delivered.” What next?',choices:[['Resume compressions immediately','c','Correct. Do not check for a pulse first.',true],['Check the pulse for 30 seconds','c','Restart compressions right away.',false]]},c:{text:'The team continues 30:2 with switches every 5 cycles until EMS takes over.',end:true}})]],
["Common AED and CPR errors on the rating sheet",["Judges watch for small errors that cost points or delay a shock. Common mistakes include **stopping compressions while the AED is being set up**, placing pads over clothing or a medication patch, **touching the patient during analysis**, forgetting to shout “**Clear!**” before the shock and **checking the pulse right after a shock** instead of resuming compressions. Another is letting the compressor continue for too long—fatigue sets in within about 2 minutes, so call the switch at 5 cycles.","Practice the AED drill until it is automatic: Rescuer 2 opens the case and powers on the unit **first**, because the device’s voice prompts guide every later step. Keep pads attached and the AED on once a shock has been given; it will reanalyze every 2 minutes. Speak every step aloud so the judge can score it, and keep interruptions in compressions to less than **10 seconds**—the rating sheet asks for less than 5 seconds during switches.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Correct AED practice?","buckets":["Correct","Error"],"items":[["Powering on the AED first",0],["Pausing compressions while pads are unpacked",1],["Shouting “Clear!” before the shock",0],["Checking the pulse right after a shock",1],["Resuming compressions immediately after a shock",0],["Touching the patient during analysis",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['AEDs treat ventricular fibrillation and pulseless VT, not asystole; use the AED as soon as it arrives and resume compressions immediately after a shock.','Skill II: Rescuer 1 compresses while Rescuer 2 powers on the AED, applies pads to the bare chest, clears, analyzes and shocks if advised, then compresses while Rescuer 1 gives bag-mask breaths; switch every 5 cycles.','Dry the chest, remove patches, keep pads about 1 inch from implanted devices, use pediatric pads under 8 years; rescue breaths: adults 1 every 6 seconds, children and infants 1 every 2–3 seconds; advanced airway: continuous compressions plus 1 breath every 6 seconds.'],terms:[['AED','Automated external defibrillator.'],['Ventricular fibrillation','A chaotic heart rhythm that pumps no blood.'],['Rescue breathing','Breaths for a person who has a pulse but is not breathing normally.']],mcqs:[q('Which rhythm does an AED treat with a shock?','Ventricular fibrillation','Asystole','Normal sinus rhythm','Bradycardia with a pulse','Also pulseless VT.'),q('What should happen immediately after a shock?','Resume chest compressions','Check the pulse for a minute','Remove the pads','Give 10 breaths','Start with compressions.'),q('Where are AED pads applied?','On the bare chest','Over clothing','Over a medication patch','On the arms','Follow the pad pictures.'),q('How far should a pad be from an implanted pacemaker?','About 1 inch','Directly on it','10 inches','It does not matter','Avoid the device.'),q('Rescue breathing rate for an adult is:','1 breath every 6 seconds','1 breath every 2 seconds','1 breath every 30 seconds','Continuous breaths','About 10 per minute.'),q('Rescue breathing rate for a child is:','1 breath every 2–3 seconds','1 breath every 6 seconds','1 breath every 10 seconds','2 breaths per minute','About 20–30 per minute.'),q('With an advanced airway in place, rescuers:','Give continuous compressions and 1 breath every 6 seconds','Stop compressions for breaths','Give 30:2','Give no breaths','No pauses.'),q('What should be done before using an AED on a person lying in water?','Move them out of the water and dry the chest','Use it in the water','Pour more water','Skip the AED','Safety.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how two rescuers integrate an AED into adult CPR following HOSA Skills II-A and II-B, and describe two special situations that change how the AED is used.',rubric:[['Initial CPR',['scene','ppe','responsive','aed','breathing','pulse','30:2','compressions']],['AED operation',['power on','pads','bare chest','clear','analyze','shock','advised']],['After the shock',['resume','compressions','immediately','bag-mask','e-c','switch','5 cycles']],['Special situations',['water','dry','patch','implanted','1 inch','child','pediatric pads','hair']]],model:'Rescuer 1 confirms scene safety, states ARC or AHA, puts on PPE, taps and shouts, sends Rescuer 2 to call 911 and bring the AED, checks breathing and the carotid pulse for 5–10 seconds and starts 30:2 CPR with high-quality compressions and pocket-mask breaths. When Rescuer 2 arrives, Rescuer 1 keeps compressing while Rescuer 2 opens the case, powers on the AED and applies the pads to the bare chest—upper right below the collarbone and lower left below the armpit—avoiding clothing, medication patches and implanted devices. Rescuer 2 clears everyone so no one touches the patient and lets the AED analyze; if a shock is advised, Rescuer 2 clears again and presses the shock button. Immediately after the shock, or if no shock is advised, Rescuer 2 begins compressions while Rescuer 1 moves to the head and gives bag-mask breaths with the E-C clamp after every 30 compressions, and the team switches roles every 5 cycles in less than 5 seconds until EMS arrives. Special situations change AED use: if the person is lying in water, we move them out and dry the chest before applying pads; if there is a medication patch, we remove it with a gloved hand and wipe the skin; if there is an implanted pacemaker, we place the pad about an inch away; and for a child younger than about 8, we use pediatric pads if available.'},sources:[RULES,AHA_CPR,ARC_AED]};

export const F4={title:'Infant and child BLS (Skill III)',hook:'In children, cardiac arrest usually follows a breathing problem—choking, drowning, severe asthma or sudden unexpected infant death—rather than a heart rhythm problem. That is why breaths matter so much in pediatric CPR. Infants are small and fragile, so technique, depth and ratios change. Skill III tests two-rescuer infant CPR.',objectives:['Compare adult, child and infant CPR.','Check infant responsiveness and the brachial pulse.','Perform infant compressions and breaths with one and two rescuers following HOSA Skills III-A and III-B.','Choose the right bag-mask size and keep the infant’s head neutral.'],sections:[
['Age groups and key differences',[
'For CPR, an **infant** is under **1 year** (excluding newborns), and a **child** is from 1 year to puberty. In children, **respiratory problems** cause most arrests, so a lone rescuer who did not witness the collapse gives about **2 minutes of CPR before leaving to call 911** if no phone is available (for a sudden witnessed collapse, call first and get an AED). Children’s and infants’ heart rates are faster; a pulse below **60 per minute with signs of poor perfusion** calls for CPR.',
'Compression depth is about **one-third of the chest depth**: about **2 inches (5 cm)** for children and **1.5 inches (4 cm)** for infants. The ratio is **30:2 for one rescuer** and **15:2 for two rescuers** in children and infants. Pulse checks use the **carotid or femoral** artery in children and the **brachial** artery in infants.',
{t:'table',caption:'Adult, child and infant BLS',head:['Feature','Adult','Child','Infant'],rows:[['Pulse check','Carotid','Carotid or femoral','Brachial'],['Compression depth','At least 2 in (5 cm)','About 2 in (1/3 chest)','About 1.5 in (1/3 chest)'],['Hands','Two hands','One or two hands','Heel of one hand or 2-thumb encircling'],['Ratio, 1 rescuer','30:2','30:2','30:2'],['Ratio, 2 rescuers','30:2','15:2','15:2'],['Rate','100–120/min','100–120/min','100–120/min']]}]],
['Assessing an infant: Skill III-A steps 1–5',[
'**1.** Verify scene safety and state ARC or AHA. **2.** Put on PPE. **3.** Check responsiveness by **tapping the bottom of the foot (heel)** and **shouting**. **4.** Shout for Rescuer 2 to activate the emergency response system and get an AED (the judge says none is available). **5.** Check for **normal breathing and a pulse** for **5–10 seconds**: under the AHA approach, scan the chest while feeling the **brachial pulse**; under the ARC approach, open the airway to a **neutral** head position first and check both at once. Find the **brachial pulse** with **2–3 fingers** on the **inside of the upper arm, midway between the elbow and shoulder**.',
'Do not shake an infant. Infants’ airways are easily blocked by tilting the head too far back, so the infant airway is opened to a **neutral (sniffing) position**. Look for normal breathing; gasping is not breathing.',
order('Infant assessment',['Scene safety; ARC or AHA','PPE','Tap the heel and shout','Send partner to call and get an AED','Check breathing and brachial pulse for 5–10 seconds'])]],
['Infant compressions and breaths: Skill III-A steps 6–8',[
'**6.** Position yourself at the infant’s **side or feet** (depending on the number of rescuers) with the infant **face up on a firm, flat surface**. Use the **heel of one hand** or the **two-thumb encircling-hands** technique at the **center of the chest just below the nipple line, on the lower half of the breastbone**. Compress **at least one-third of the chest depth, about 1.5 inches (4 cm)**, at **100–120 per minute**—**30 compressions in 15–18 seconds**—counting aloud and allowing full recoil.',
'**7.** Place the mask using the bridge of the nose as a guide, open the airway to a **neutral** position and give **2 breaths over 1 second each** to make the chest rise—gentle breaths, just enough for visible rise. **8.** Continue **30:2** with interruptions under 5 seconds until Rescuer 2 arrives, continuing compressions.',
chk('What compression depth is used for an infant?',['At least one-third of the chest depth, about 1.5 inches (4 cm)','At least 2.5 inches','Half an inch','As deep as possible'],'Child: about 2 inches; adult: at least 2 inches.')]],
['Two-rescuer infant CPR: Skill III-A steps 9–15 and III-B',[
'When Rescuer 2 arrives: **9.** the compressor **changes to the two-thumb encircling technique** (if not already using it) and the ratio changes to **15:2**. Rescuer 2 (after treating the first aid patient and changing gloves) arrives with a **bag-mask**, takes position **directly above the head** and uses an **appropriately sized mask**—covering the **mouth and nose** completely **without covering the eyes or extending below the chin**. Using the **E-C clamp**, place the infant’s neck in a **neutral position**, seal the mask and give **2 breaths over 1 second each after every 15 compressions**, watching for chest rise, with no more than 5 seconds between sets.',
'After **5 cycles**, switch roles in **less than 5 seconds**. As compressor, Rescuer 2 positions at the **infant’s feet** and uses the two-thumb encircling technique, **15 compressions in 7–9 seconds**, at least one-third chest depth. Continue **15:2** until the judge says EMS has taken over, then report, remove gloves properly, dispose of PPE and use hand rub.',
order('Two-rescuer infant CPR',['Compressor switches to two-thumb encircling','Ratio changes to 15:2','Ventilator above the head with the correct-size bag-mask','E-C clamp with the neck neutral','2 breaths after every 15 compressions','Switch every 5 cycles in under 5 seconds'])]],
['Child CPR and prevention',[
'For a **child**, use **one or two hands** on the lower half of the breastbone, compressing about **2 inches** (one-third of chest depth) at 100–120 per minute, **30:2** for one rescuer and **15:2** for two. Open the airway with a slight head tilt (more than an infant, less than an adult). Use the AED with pediatric pads when available. Many pediatric arrests are preventable: safe sleep for infants (**alone, on the back, in a crib**), pool fencing and supervision, car seats, choking prevention and asthma management.',
kase('Infant emergency',{a:{text:'An infant is unresponsive and not breathing, with no brachial pulse. You are alone with a phone. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Call 911 on speaker and begin 30:2 CPR','b','Correct. A lone rescuer with a phone can call and start CPR.',true],['Shake the infant to wake them','b','Never shake an infant.',false]]},b:{text:'A second rescuer arrives with an infant bag-mask. What changes?',choices:[['Switch to the two-thumb encircling technique and a 15:2 ratio','c','Correct. Two-rescuer infant CPR.',true],['Keep 30:2 and the same technique','c','The ratio changes with two rescuers.',false]]},c:{text:'You continue 15:2 with switches every 5 cycles until EMS arrives.',end:true}})]],
["Pediatric CPR mistakes to avoid",["The most common pediatric errors are compressing **too shallowly** out of fear of injuring a small chest, tilting an infant’s head **too far back** (which can kink the soft airway), giving **too-forceful breaths** and forgetting to change the ratio to **15:2** when the second rescuer arrives. A properly sized mask is also critical: a mask that covers the eyes or slips below the chin will not seal.","Remember that infants and children rarely arrest from a heart rhythm problem. Effective breaths are therefore essential, and drowning, choking, sudden unexpected infant death and severe asthma are common causes. Teach families **safe sleep**—alone, on the back, in a crib with no blankets, pillows or bumpers—and keep coins, button batteries and small toys out of reach.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Correct pediatric practice?","buckets":["Correct","Error"],"items":[["Neutral head position for an infant",0],["Compressing an infant only half an inch",1],["Switching to 15:2 with two rescuers",0],["A mask that covers the infant’s eyes",1],["Gentle breaths that just make the chest rise",0],["Checking the carotid pulse in an infant",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Infant: under 1 year; child: 1 year to puberty; pediatric arrests are usually respiratory; depth one-third of the chest (child about 2 in, infant about 1.5 in); 30:2 one rescuer, 15:2 two rescuers.','Infant assessment: tap the heel and shout, check breathing and the brachial pulse (inside upper arm) for 5–10 seconds, neutral head position.','Infant compressions just below the nipple line with the heel of one hand or two-thumb encircling, 30 in 15–18 seconds (15 in 7–9 seconds with two rescuers); correct-size bag-mask covering mouth and nose but not eyes; switch every 5 cycles.'],terms:[['Brachial pulse','The pulse on the inside of an infant’s upper arm.'],['Two-thumb encircling technique','Infant compressions with both thumbs while the hands encircle the chest.'],['Neutral position','A head position that opens an infant’s airway without over-tilting.']],mcqs:[q('Where is an infant’s pulse checked?','Brachial artery','Carotid artery','Radial artery','Temporal artery','Inside the upper arm.'),q('How do you check an infant’s responsiveness?','Tap the bottom of the foot and shout','Shake vigorously','Pinch the ear','Clap loudly only','Never shake.'),q('What is the two-rescuer ratio for infants?','15:2','30:2','5:1','3:1','Single rescuer is 30:2.'),q('Infant compression depth is about:','1.5 inches (4 cm)','2.5 inches','Half an inch','3 inches','One-third of the chest.'),q('Where are infant compressions given?','Just below the nipple line on the lower half of the breastbone','On the upper chest','On the abdomen','On the left side','Center of the chest.'),q('How should a correctly sized infant mask fit?','Over the mouth and nose without covering the eyes or extending below the chin','Over the whole face','Only over the mouth','Over the eyes','Proper seal.'),q('In children, cardiac arrest most often follows:','A breathing problem','A heart rhythm problem','A broken bone','A rash','Respiratory causes.'),q('How many compressions are given in 7–9 seconds during two-rescuer infant CPR?','15','30','5','60','100–120/min.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare adult and infant CPR, and describe how two rescuers perform infant CPR following HOSA Skills III-A and III-B.',rubric:[['Differences',['brachial','carotid','1.5 inches','2 inches','one-third','15:2','30:2','respiratory']],['Assessment',['scene','ppe','tap','heel','shout','activate','brachial','5-10 seconds','neutral']],['Technique',['firm','flat','nipple line','heel of one hand','two-thumb','100-120','recoil','2 breaths','1 second']],['Two rescuers',['bag-mask','size','e-c','15 compressions','switch','5 cycles','5 seconds','ems']]],model:'Infant CPR differs from adult CPR in several ways: the pulse is checked at the brachial artery instead of the carotid; compressions are about 1.5 inches, or one-third of the chest depth, instead of at least 2 inches, using the heel of one hand or the two-thumb encircling technique rather than two hands; the airway is opened only to a neutral position; and two rescuers use a 15:2 ratio instead of 30:2, because pediatric arrests are usually caused by breathing problems. Rescuer 1 confirms scene safety, states ARC or AHA, puts on PPE, taps the infant’s heel and shouts, then sends Rescuer 2 to call 911 and get an AED. Rescuer 1 checks breathing and the brachial pulse—two or three fingers inside the upper arm between the elbow and shoulder—for 5–10 seconds, finds neither and places the infant face up on a firm surface. Rescuer 1 compresses at the center of the chest just below the nipple line, 30 times in 15–18 seconds, at least one-third of the chest depth with full recoil, then gives 2 gentle breaths over 1 second each with a mask, continuing 30:2. When Rescuer 2 arrives with an infant bag-mask, Rescuer 1 switches to the two-thumb encircling technique and a 15:2 ratio. Rescuer 2, above the head, chooses a mask that covers the mouth and nose without covering the eyes or extending below the chin, keeps the neck neutral, seals it with the E-C clamp and gives 2 breaths after every 15 compressions. They switch every 5 cycles in under 5 seconds until EMS takes over.'},sources:[RULES,AHA_CPR,ARC_CHILD]};

export const F5={title:'Choking and breathing emergencies (Skill V)',hook:'Choking is a leading cause of injury death in young children and a common emergency in older adults. A person with a completely blocked airway can lose consciousness in minutes, but back blows and abdominal thrusts usually clear the object. Skill V tests relief of choking in a conscious adult or child, and the 2026 update made the technique the same for AHA and ARC.',objectives:['Distinguish mild from severe airway obstruction.','Relieve choking in a conscious adult or child using back blows and abdominal thrusts following HOSA Skill V.','Modify choking care for infants, pregnant and large people and those who become unresponsive.','Recognize and assist with asthma and other breathing emergencies.'],sections:[
['Recognizing choking',[
'With a **mild airway obstruction**, the person can **cough forcefully**, speak and breathe; encourage them to keep coughing and stay with them. With a **severe obstruction**, the person **cannot speak, cough effectively or breathe**, may make high-pitched noises or none, may turn blue and often clutches the throat—the **universal choking sign**. Severe obstruction requires immediate action.',
'Common causes include food (especially hot dogs, grapes, nuts, popcorn and hard candy in young children), small toys, coins and balloons. Older adults and people with swallowing problems are also at risk. Cutting food into small pieces, supervising young children and keeping small objects out of reach prevent many choking events.',
sort('Mild or severe obstruction?',['Mild—encourage coughing','Severe—act now'],[['Coughing forcefully and talking',0],['Cannot speak or cough',1],['Clutching the throat with no sound',1],['Wheezing but able to answer',0],['Turning blue and unable to breathe',1]])]],
['Relieving choking: Skill V steps 1–8',[
'Skill V uses a sitting or standing patient who is **not pregnant or very large**. **1.** Observe and **verbalize the signs of severe airway obstruction**. **2.** Ask, “**Are you choking? Can I help you?**” (the patient nods but cannot talk). **3.** Respond to Rescuer 1’s call for help (this is Rescuer 2’s first aid skill). **4. Back blows:** **a.** stand **to the side and slightly behind** the patient; **b.** support them with **one arm diagonally across the chest**; **c.** bend them **forward at the waist** so the upper body is close to **parallel with the ground**; **d.** with the **heel of your hand**, give **5 firm back blows between the shoulder blades**, each a separate attempt to dislodge the object.',
'**5.** Stand or kneel behind the patient and **wrap your arms around the waist**. **6.** Make a **fist** with one hand. **7.** Place the **thumb side of the fist** against the abdomen **in the midline, slightly above the navel and well below the breastbone**, grasp it with the other hand. **8.** **Alternate 5 back blows with 5 abdominal thrusts** (simulated) until the object comes out. Each abdominal thrust is a quick **inward and upward** movement. **9.** Then call EMS (name, location, phone, situation and patients; hang up when told). **10.** Put on PPE or change gloves. **11.** Assist Rescuer 1 with BLS.',
order('Choking relief',['Recognize severe obstruction','Ask “Are you choking? Can I help?”','Stand to the side and slightly behind; support the chest','Bend forward; 5 back blows between the shoulder blades','Arms around the waist; fist above the navel','5 abdominal thrusts inward and upward','Alternate 5 and 5 until the object comes out','Call EMS; change gloves; help with BLS'])]],
['Special situations',[
'**Pregnant** or **very large** people: give **chest thrusts** instead of abdominal thrusts, with your fist on the center of the breastbone. **Infants** (under 1 year): hold the infant face down along your forearm with the head lower than the chest, supporting the jaw, and give **5 back blows** between the shoulder blades; turn the infant face up and give **5 chest thrusts** with two fingers or the heel of one hand on the breastbone just below the nipple line; repeat. **Never** give abdominal thrusts to an infant, and never do blind finger sweeps.',
'If the person becomes **unresponsive**, lower them carefully to the ground, call 911 if not done and **begin CPR** starting with compressions. Each time you open the airway to give breaths, **look in the mouth** and remove an object only if you can **see** it. Anyone who received abdominal thrusts should be checked by a healthcare provider because thrusts can cause internal injury. If you are alone and choking, give yourself abdominal thrusts or thrust your abdomen against the back of a chair.',
match('Patient and technique',[['Adult or child','Alternate 5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts'],['Pregnant or very large person','Chest thrusts'],['Infant','5 back blows and 5 chest thrusts'],['Becomes unresponsive','Lower to the ground and begin CPR'],['Object visible in the mouth','Remove it'],['Choking alone','Self-thrusts or a chair back']])]],
['Asthma and other breathing emergencies',[
'**Asthma attacks** cause wheezing, coughing, chest tightness and shortness of breath. Help the person sit upright and use their **quick-relief (rescue) inhaler**, ideally with a **spacer**, following the prescribed dose; call 911 if breathing does not improve, they cannot speak in full sentences, lips turn blue or they become drowsy. **Hyperventilation** from anxiety causes rapid breathing and tingling; calm the person, but consider medical causes.',
'Other breathing emergencies include **anaphylaxis** (swelling of the face and throat—give epinephrine), **pneumonia**, **COPD** flare-ups, **drowning**, **smoke inhalation** and **opioid overdose**. Signs that breathing is failing include very fast or slow breathing, blue lips, confusion and inability to speak. Keep the person in the most comfortable position (usually sitting up), loosen tight clothing and be ready to give rescue breaths or CPR.',
order('Assisting with a rescue inhaler',['Help the person sit upright','Remove the inhaler cap and shake it','Attach a spacer if available','Person breathes out fully','Press the inhaler as they breathe in slowly','Hold the breath, then repeat as prescribed; call 911 if no improvement'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Choking at lunch',{a:{text:'A student clutches their throat and cannot speak or cough. They nod when you ask if they are choking. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do first?',choices:[['Stand to the side and slightly behind, support the chest and give 5 back blows','b','Correct. Then 5 abdominal thrusts.',true],['Give them water','b','Water can worsen a complete obstruction.',false]]},b:{text:'After 5 back blows the object is still stuck. What next?',choices:[['Give 5 abdominal thrusts and keep alternating','c','Correct. Alternate 5 and 5.',true],['Wait to see if it clears','c','Severe obstruction needs continued action.',false]]},c:{text:'The object comes out. You call EMS, change gloves and help your partner with the BLS patient.',end:true}})]],
["Preventing choking and airway emergencies",["Most choking is preventable. For **young children**, cut grapes and hot dogs lengthwise into small pieces, avoid hard candy, popcorn and whole nuts before about age 4, keep balloons, coins, magnets and **button batteries** away and make sure children sit while eating. For **older adults** and people with swallowing problems (dysphagia), serve soft foods, encourage small bites and sitting upright, and avoid talking or laughing while chewing.","Alcohol, poorly fitting dentures and eating too fast also increase risk. Learn the signs and practice the technique with a partner (simulating thrusts, never performing them on a person who is not choking). In the event, Rescuer 2 must **verbalize** the signs of severe obstruction and **simulate** the abdominal thrusts on the patient-actor; the back blows are part of the skill under the 2026 AHA and ARC guidelines.",{"t":"check","q":"Which food should be cut lengthwise into small pieces for a toddler?","options":["Grapes","Mashed potatoes","Yogurt","Applesauce"],"why":"Round foods like grapes and hot dogs can plug a child’s airway."}]]
],takeaways:['Mild obstruction: encourage coughing; severe obstruction (cannot speak, cough or breathe; universal sign): act immediately.','Skill V: ask “Are you choking? Can I help?”; stand to the side and slightly behind, support the chest, bend forward, 5 back blows with the heel of the hand; then fist slightly above the navel and 5 abdominal thrusts; alternate until expelled; call EMS.','Chest thrusts for pregnant or very large people; infants get 5 back blows and 5 chest thrusts; unresponsive → CPR and look in the mouth; help with rescue inhalers for asthma.'],terms:[['Severe airway obstruction','A blockage that prevents speaking, coughing or breathing.'],['Abdominal thrusts','Quick inward and upward thrusts above the navel to expel an object.'],['Universal choking sign','Clutching the throat with one or both hands.']],mcqs:[q('Which sign shows a severe airway obstruction?','The person cannot speak or cough','The person coughs forcefully','The person talks normally','The person is laughing','Act immediately.'),q('Where are back blows given?','Between the shoulder blades','On the lower back','On the chest','On the head','Heel of the hand.'),q('How many back blows are alternated with abdominal thrusts?','5','1','10','20','5 and 5.'),q('Where is the fist placed for abdominal thrusts?','In the midline slightly above the navel, well below the breastbone','On the breastbone','Below the navel','On the side','Thumb side in.'),q('Which technique is used for a pregnant woman who is choking?','Chest thrusts','Abdominal thrusts','Finger sweeps','No action','Avoid the abdomen.'),q('Which technique is used for a choking infant?','5 back blows and 5 chest thrusts','Abdominal thrusts','Blind finger sweeps','Shaking','Never abdominal thrusts.'),q('If a choking person becomes unresponsive, you should:','Lower them to the ground and begin CPR','Continue abdominal thrusts standing','Leave to find help first','Give water','Look in the mouth before breaths.'),q('A person having an asthma attack should be helped to:','Sit upright and use their rescue inhaler','Lie flat','Run outside','Hold their breath','Call 911 if no improvement.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to relieve severe choking in a conscious adult following HOSA Skill V, and explain how the technique changes for an infant and for a pregnant woman.',rubric:[['Recognition and consent',['cannot speak','cough','breathe','universal','are you choking','can i help']],['Back blows',['side','behind','arm','chest','bend forward','parallel','heel','5','shoulder blades']],['Abdominal thrusts',['wrap','waist','fist','thumb side','above the navel','below the breastbone','inward','upward','alternate']],['Modifications and follow-up',['infant','chest thrusts','back blows','pregnant','breastbone','unresponsive','cpr','ems']]],model:'I recognize severe choking when the person cannot speak, cough effectively or breathe and is clutching the throat. I ask, “Are you choking? Can I help you?” and when they nod, I act. I stand to the side and slightly behind them, place one arm diagonally across their chest for support and bend them forward at the waist until the upper body is nearly parallel to the ground. With the heel of my hand, I give 5 firm, separate back blows between the shoulder blades. If the object is still stuck, I stand behind them, wrap my arms around the waist, make a fist and place its thumb side in the midline slightly above the navel and well below the breastbone, grasp it with my other hand and give 5 quick inward and upward abdominal thrusts. I keep alternating 5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts until the object comes out or the person becomes unresponsive; then I call EMS with my name, location, phone number and the situation, and recommend a medical check. For an infant, I hold the baby face down along my forearm with the head lower than the chest and give 5 back blows, then turn the infant face up and give 5 chest thrusts on the breastbone just below the nipple line—never abdominal thrusts. For a pregnant woman, I give chest thrusts with my fist on the center of the breastbone instead of abdominal thrusts. If anyone becomes unresponsive, I lower them to the ground, begin CPR and look in the mouth before giving breaths.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_CHOKE,MEDLINE_CHOKE_INF,AHA_CPR]};
